dante 1.4.4 libdsocks: concurrent connect-child, and drain all replies in sigio()

Applies to lib/connectchild.c of dante 1.4.4 (upstream tarball, also on top
of Alpine's ifdev, gaierrval and dante-no-bindresvport patches).

1. For a non-blocking connect(2), libdsocks connects to the proxy, passes the
   socket to a forked "connect-child" over a unix datagram socketpair and
   returns EINPROGRESS. The connect-child negotiates each request serially,
   in blocking mode. When the proxy is slow to answer CONNECT (seconds, e.g.
   an anonymizing network or a distant proxy), the datagram queue from the
   mother fills, and the mother's sendmsgn() of the next request blocks
   without a timeout: the application thread that called connect(2) hangs.
   In an event-loop program that is the loop thread, so the whole program
   stops responding while it sits idle.
   The connect-child now forks one worker per request:
   - at most SOCKS_CONNECTCHILD_MAXWORKERS (default 128) at a time; past
     that, the connect fails immediately (ECONNREFUSED, socket shut down)
     instead of queueing;
   - a worker gives up after SOCKS_CONNECTCHILD_TIMEOUT seconds (default
     30): SIGALRM shuts the socket down, so the blocking negotiation reads
     EOF and the normal failure reply goes to the mother.
   If fork(2) fails, the request is negotiated inline as before.

2. sigio() stopped reading replies after the first failed one. SIGIOs
   coalesce, so replies that arrived together with it stayed queued until
   another reply raised SIGIO, and their connects stayed "in progress"
   forever. It now continues with the next reply. Serial negotiation spaces
   replies out and mostly hides this; concurrent workers make it common.

Test: test/run.sh.

--- a/lib/connectchild.c
+++ b/lib/connectchild.c
@@ -83,6 +83,24 @@
 static struct sigaction       originalhandler;
 static volatile sig_atomic_t  reqoutstanding;
 
+/*
+ * Concurrent connect-child.
+ * Upstream negotiates every request serially in the connect-child, and the
+ * mother's sendmsgn() of a new request blocks without a timeout once the
+ * datagram queue to the child is full.  With a slow proxy the queue fills,
+ * and the thread doing the non-blocking connect(2) hangs.  Instead,
+ * the connect-child forks a worker per request (at most
+ * SOCKS_CONNECTCHILD_MAXWORKERS, default 128; beyond that the connect fails
+ * at once), and each worker gives up after SOCKS_CONNECTCHILD_TIMEOUT
+ * seconds (default 30).
+ */
+static volatile sig_atomic_t  connectworkers;
+static int                    connectworkers_max     = 128;
+static int                    connectworker_timeout  = 30;
+static int                    connectworker_control  = -1;
+static void connectworker_reap(int sig);
+static void connectworker_alarm(int sig);
+
 route_t *
 socks_nbconnectroute(s, control, packet, src, dst, emsg, emsglen)
    int s;
